The central government of '''[[Okaiken]]''' consists of legislative, executive and judiciary branches and functions under the framework established by the Constitution of Okaiken. Okaiken is a unitary state, containing forty-seven administrative divisions, with the emperor as its head of state. Following the Jutsu Restoration and the subsequent referendums of 2011, the emperor is formally defined as the head of state, sharing executive authority with the cabinet. While the throne possesses significant reserve powers, day-to-day governance is directed and controlled by the cabinet, comprising the prime minister and the ministers of state. The cabinet holds the executive power and is led by the prime minister, who is the head of government. The prime minister is nominated by the National Diet and appointed to office by the emperor. The current cabinet is the second Honda cabinet, led by prime minister Honda Shigetsuma, who formed a new coalition government with the Okaiken Socialist Party following the 2025 general election.

The National Diet is the legislature and the organ of the legislative branch. The Diet is bicameral, consisting of two houses: the House of Officers being the upper house, and the House of Commoners being the lower house. The members of both houses are directly elected by the people, who are the source of sovereignty. The Diet is defined as the supreme organ of state power in the constitution. The Supreme Court and lower courts make up the judicial branch and hold all judicial powers. The Supreme Court has ultimate authority to interpret the constitution and possesses the power of judicial review. The judicial branch is independent from the executive and legislative branches. Judges are nominated or appointed by the cabinet and can only be removed through public impeachment or if declared mentally or physically incompetent.

The government of Okaiken is based in the capital of Okami, where the National Diet Building, the Imperial Palace, the Supreme Court, the Prime Minister's Office and the ministries are located.

== History ==
Before the modern era, Okaiken was ruled by the government of successive military shoguns of the Honda clan. During this period, effective power resided in the shogun, who ruled the country in the name of the emperor. The shogun was the hereditary military governor, while the emperor remained a ceremonial sovereign with no part in governing the country. This historical period established a philosophy of dual sovereignty that influenced the modern restoration of imperial powers.

The return of political power to the emperor at the end of the 19th century restored imperial rule and led to the proclamation of the Empire of Okaiken. This period saw the adoption of a semi-constitutional monarchy based on the civil law models of Posillipo. A new aristocracy known as the kazoku was established, merging ancient court nobility with former feudal lords. The first parliament consisted of a House of Commoners and a House of Peers.

Following the Quiet Revolution of 1968, the modern constitution of 1970 was adopted, replacing the semi-aristocratic system with a liberal democracy and converting the House of Peers into the democratized House of Officers. However, the political landscape shifted again with the referendums of 2011. Known as the Jutsu Restoration, these amendments restored the emperor as the official head of state and granted the throne reserve powers to arbitrate parliamentary deadlocks and command the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces in emergencies.

== The Emperor ==
{{Politics of Okaiken}}
{{Main|Emperor of Okaiken}}
The emperor of Okaiken is the head of the imperial family and the official head of state. He is defined by the constitution as the symbol of the state and the unity of the people. Following the 2011 amendments, the emperor transitioned from a purely symbolic figure to a head of state with specific reserve powers.

The ceremonial roles of the emperor include:
# Appointment of the prime minister as designated by the Diet.
# Appointment of the chief justice of the Supreme Court as designated by the cabinet.
# Promulgation of amendments, laws, cabinet orders, and treaties.
# Dissolution of the House of Commoners.
# Proclamation of general elections.
# Awarding of honors and receiving foreign ambassadors.

While day-to-day executive power is exercised by the prime minister, the emperor acts as an active participant in governance during national emergencies or legislative crises. The throne is hereditary and descends in the male line. The current sovereign is emperor Masahito, who has reigned since 1985 and transitioned to the head of state role in 2011. His reign bears the era name Jutsu.

== Executive ==
The executive branch is headed by the prime minister, who is the head of the cabinet and is designated by the National Diet. The cabinet consists of the ministers of state and is responsible to the Diet. If the cabinet loses the confidence of the Diet, it may be dismissed en masse through a motion of no confidence.

=== Prime minister ===
{{Main|Prime Minister of Okaiken}}
The prime minister of Okaiken is designated by the National Diet and serves a term of four years or less. The prime minister heads the cabinet, exercises control over the executive branch, and serves as the commander-in-chief of the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces. Vested powers include presenting bills to the Diet, signing laws, and dissolving the House of Commoners at will. The current prime minister is Honda Shigetsuma, leader of the Progressive Party of Okaiken.

=== The Cabinet ===
{{Main|Cabinet of Okaiken}}
The cabinet consists of the prime minister and the ministers of state. Members are appointed by the prime minister and must be civilians. A majority must be members of the National Diet. The cabinet is responsible for administering the law, managing foreign affairs, concluding treaties with Diet approval, and preparing the national budget. The current cabinet is a coalition formed between the Progressive Party of Okaiken and the Okaiken Socialist Party.

== Legislative ==
The legislative organ is the National Diet, a bicameral parliament consisting of the House of Commoners (lower house) and the House of Officers (upper house). It is defined as the highest organ of state power and the sole law-making organ. Both houses are elected by universal suffrage for adults aged 18 and over under a secret ballot.

=== House of Commoners ===
{{Main|House of Commoners of Okaiken}}
The House of Commoners consists of 475 members elected for a term of up to four years. Seats are allocated using the Direct Party and Representative (DPR) voting system. The House of Commoners is the more powerful house, possessing supremacy over the budget and treaties. It can override decisions by the upper house with a two-thirds majority.

=== House of Officers ===
{{Main|House of Officers of Okaiken}}

The House of Officers consists of 251 members serving fixed six-year terms, with half elected every three years. While it cannot be dissolved by the prime minister, it serves as a chamber of review. Before 1968, it was an aristocratic body, but it was democratized by the 1970 constitution.

== Judicial ==
The judicial branch consists of the Supreme Court and three levels of lower courts: high courts, district courts, and family or summary courts. The independence of the judiciary is guaranteed by the constitution. Judges are independent and bound only by the constitution and the laws.

=== Supreme Court ===
The Supreme Court of Okaiken is the court of last resort and possesses the power of judicial review to determine the constitutionality of laws. Justices are appointed by the cabinet and are subject to a people's review referendum during general elections.

=== Legal system ===
Historically influenced by Zongese law, the Sanese legal system developed through texts like the Kujikata Osadamegaki during the shogunate. Since the late 19th century, it has been based on the civil law of Posillipo. The main body of statutory law is called the Six Codes. Laws require the imperial seal and the countersignature of the prime minister to take effect.

== Local government ==
Local governments are public entities defined by the principle of local autonomy. Okaiken is divided into forty-seven administrative divisions, including the Okami Metropolis and various prefectures. Governors and mayors are directly elected by residents. Local authorities have limited legislative and executive powers to manage regional affairs, health, and welfare.

